<comment datatype='string'>Crotonase is an enzyme in the central fermentation pathway of &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-1488&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Clostridium acetobutylicum&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; that plays a central role in the production of both acids and solvents. 
The enzyme been purified to homogeneity from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-1488&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Clostridium acetobutylicum&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and found to be a homotetramer. The enzyme was highly specific for short, C4-C6, chain length substrates [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/5057466&quot;&gt;Waterson72&lt;/a&gt;]. The enzyme exhibits an extremely high turnover number for &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CROTONYL-COA&quot;&gt;crotonyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; (Vmax = 6.5 x 10&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;/sup&gt; mol per minute per mole of enzyme), and is markedly sensitive to inhibition by crotonyl-CoA at concentrations greater than about 75 &amp;mu;M [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/7024731&quot;&gt;Waterson81&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-98&quot;&gt;crt&lt;/a&gt; gene encoding the enzyme has been cloned and sequenced [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/8655474&quot;&gt;Boynton96&lt;/a&gt;].
While earlier work reported that the subunit size is 43 kDa, and thus that the protein is a homotetramer [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/5057466&quot;&gt;Waterson72&lt;/a&gt;], the size of the recombinant monomer was found to be 29 kDa [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/8655474&quot;&gt;Boynton96&lt;/a&gt;], and thus the protein is most likely a hexamer, similar to the bovine enzyme.</comment>
